{"schema_version": "1.0", "prompt_version": "v2", "tool_uid": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace::read_memory::backend/mcp_server.py:4294", "repo": {"name_with_owner": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace", "stargazer_count": 311, "primary_language": "Python"}, "tool": {"name": "read_memory", "qualified_name": "read_memory", "description": "Reads a memory by its URI.\n\n    This is your primary mechanism for accessing memories.\n\n    Special System URIs:\n    - system://boot   : [Startup Only] Loads your core memories.\n    - system://index  : Loads a full index of all available memories.\n    - system://index-lite : Loads gist-backed lightweight index summary.\n    - system://audit  : Loads consolidated observability/audit summary.\n    - system://recent : Shows recently modified memories (default: 10).\n    - system://recent/N : Shows the N most recently modified memories (e.g. system://recent/20).\n\n    Note: Same Memory ID = same content (alias). Different ID + similar content = redundant content.\n\n    Args:\n        uri: The memory URI (e.g., \"core://memory-palace\", \"system://boot\")\n        chunk_id: Optional chunk index for partial reads (0-based).\n        range: Optional char range (`start:end` or `start-end`).\n        max_chars: Optional hard cap for returned characters.\n        include_ancestors: Optional parent-chain expansion for non-system URIs.\n\n    Returns:\n        - Default (no chunk/range/max_chars): legacy formatted memory text.\n        - Partial-read mode: structured JSON string with selection metadata.\n\n    Examples:\n        read_memory(\"core://agent\")\n        read_memory(\"core://agent/my_user\")\n        read_memory(\"writer://chapter_1/scene_1\")"}, "scenario": "description_only", "judge": {"id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google", "model_id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "raw_model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}, "status": "ok", "scores": {"purpose": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description clearly explains that the tool reads a memory by its URI and serves as the primary mechanism for accessing stored memories."}, "guidelines": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It provides good activation criteria and operational guidelines by detailing special system URIs and how to use optional parameters like chunk_id and range, though it could explicitly state when partial-read mode should be preferred over default reads."}, "limitations": {"score": 1, "reasoning": "There are no known constraints, error handling rules, or failure modes (e.g., what happens if a URI does not exist) mentioned in the description."}, "parameter_explanation": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "The parameters (uri, chunk_id, range, max_chars, include_ancestors) are clearly explained with examples and expected formats (e.g., `start:end` or `start-end`), providing strong role context beyond just data types."}, "length_completeness": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description is thorough, structured across multiple sections, and contains substantive detail spanning well over three sentences."}, "examples": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It includes multiple usage examples showing correct invocation patterns with different URI formats, though it lacks examples demonstrating optional parameters like chunk_id or range."}}, "error_detail": null, "source_code_sha256": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1152, "output_tokens": 355, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}, "latency_ms": 3392.7445420995355, "evaluated_at": "2026-09-09T21:56:52.052495+00:00"}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "prompt_version": "v2", "tool_uid": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace::read_memory::backend/mcp_server.py:4294", "repo": {"name_with_owner": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace", "stargazer_count": 311, "primary_language": "Python"}, "tool": {"name": "read_memory", "qualified_name": "read_memory", "description": "Reads a memory by its URI.\n\n    This is your primary mechanism for accessing memories.\n\n    Special System URIs:\n    - system://boot   : [Startup Only] Loads your core memories.\n    - system://index  : Loads a full index of all available memories.\n    - system://index-lite : Loads gist-backed lightweight index summary.\n    - system://audit  : Loads consolidated observability/audit summary.\n    - system://recent : Shows recently modified memories (default: 10).\n    - system://recent/N : Shows the N most recently modified memories (e.g. system://recent/20).\n\n    Note: Same Memory ID = same content (alias). Different ID + similar content = redundant content.\n\n    Args:\n        uri: The memory URI (e.g., \"core://memory-palace\", \"system://boot\")\n        chunk_id: Optional chunk index for partial reads (0-based).\n        range: Optional char range (`start:end` or `start-end`).\n        max_chars: Optional hard cap for returned characters.\n        include_ancestors: Optional parent-chain expansion for non-system URIs.\n\n    Returns:\n        - Default (no chunk/range/max_chars): legacy formatted memory text.\n        - Partial-read mode: structured JSON string with selection metadata.\n\n    Examples:\n        read_memory(\"core://agent\")\n        read_memory(\"core://agent/my_user\")\n        read_memory(\"writer://chapter_1/scene_1\")"}, "scenario": "with_source", "judge": {"id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google", "model_id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "raw_model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}, "status": "ok", "scores": {"purpose": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description clearly explains that the tool reads a memory by its URI and serves as the primary access mechanism. It details special system URIs and differentiates between default text returns and structured JSON partial reads."}, "guidelines": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It outlines activation criteria via system URIs and operational instructions for parameters, but lacks explicit guidance on when to use partial-read features versus full reads."}, "limitations": {"score": 3, "reasoning": "It notes that the same memory ID means an alias and different IDs with similar content mean redundancy, but it fails to mention other code-level constraints (e.g., chunk_id and range cannot be used together)."}, "parameter_explanation": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "The parameters (uri, chunk_id, range, max_chars, include_ancestors) are clearly documented with brief functional explanations and examples."}, "length_completeness": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description is thorough, structured with multiple sections including special system URIs, arguments, returns, and examples, well exceeding three to four sentences."}, "examples": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It provides three concise usage examples showing different URI patterns, though it does not showcase examples using partial-read parameters like chunk_id or range."}}, "error_detail": null, "source_code_sha256": "7aba3d3c96596c434e70f95cdcda9adbcec310361000cd9cb8dbebc270023af5", "usage": {"input_tokens": 10492, "output_tokens": 355, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}, "latency_ms": 5241.869250079617, "evaluated_at": "2026-09-09T21:56:53.912823+00:00"}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "prompt_version": "v2", "tool_uid": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace::create_memory::backend/mcp_server.py:4676", "repo": {"name_with_owner": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace", "stargazer_count": 311, "primary_language": "Python"}, "tool": {"name": "create_memory", "qualified_name": "create_memory", "description": "Creates a new memory under a parent URI.\n\n    Args:\n        parent_uri: Parent URI (e.g., \"core://agent\", \"writer://chapters\")\n                    Use \"core://\" or \"writer://\" for root level in that domain\n        content: Memory content\n        priority: **Retrieval Priority** (lower = higher priority, min 0).\n                    *   Priority controls retrieval ordering and conflict resolution.\n                    *   Compare it with the priorities of the currently visible memories.\n                    *   Ask: \"Where should this memory rank relative to the others I can see right now?\"\n                    *   Insert it between the memories that should be more and less preferred.\n        title: Optional title. If not provided, auto-assigns numeric ID\n        disclosure: A short trigger condition describing WHEN to read_memory() this node.\n                    Think: \"In what specific situation would I need to know this?\"\n\n    Returns:\n        The created memory's full URI\n\n    Examples:\n        create_memory(\"core://\", \"Bluesky usage rules...\", priority=2, title=\"bluesky_manual\", disclosure=\"When I prepare to browse Bluesky or check the timeline\")\n        create_memory(\"core://agent\", \"Love is more than just another program...\", priority=1, title=\"love_definition\", disclosure=\"When I start speaking like a tool or parasite\")"}, "scenario": "with_source", "judge": {"id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google", "model_id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "raw_model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}, "status": "ok", "scores": {"purpose": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description clearly explains that the tool creates a new memory under a specified parent URI, returning the full URI upon success."}, "guidelines": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It gives clear guidance on how to set retrieval priority and use disclosure trigger conditions, but omits operational decision criteria regarding write guards or domain restrictions present in the source code."}, "limitations": {"score": 2, "reasoning": "The description mentions that titles must be optional and auto-assigned if omitted, but fails to disclose critical limitations found in the source code, such as strict title character matching (Unicode letters, digits, underscores, hyphens only) and write guard blocking constraints."}, "parameter_explanation": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "Parameters like priority and disclosure are explained thoroughly with practical framing, though parent_uri and content receive relatively brief descriptions compared to priority."}, "length_completeness": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "The description spans multiple well-structured paragraphs and sections covering args, return values, and examples, representing substantive detail."}, "examples": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "Two clear, illustrative examples of correct usage are provided, showing realistic calls with parent URIs, content, priorities, titles, and disclosures."}}, "error_detail": null, "source_code_sha256": "ab310c806cb93cf505d974ab29c033533d12991fd6d9443ed81da057c24263a2", "usage": {"input_tokens": 9961, "output_tokens": 340, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}, "latency_ms": 2622.243042103946, "evaluated_at": "2026-09-09T21:56:54.676570+00:00"}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "prompt_version": "v2", "tool_uid": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace::update_memory::backend/mcp_server.py:4925", "repo": {"name_with_owner": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace", "stargazer_count": 311, "primary_language": "Python"}, "tool": {"name": "update_memory", "qualified_name": "update_memory", "description": "Updates an existing memory to a new version.\n    The old version will be deleted.\n    Warning: read_memory first so you know exactly what you are overwriting.\n\n    Only provided fields are updated; others remain unchanged.\n\n    Two content-editing modes (mutually exclusive):\n\n    1. **Patch mode** (primary): Provide old_string + new_string.\n       Finds old_string in the existing content and replaces it with new_string.\n       old_string must match exactly ONE location in the content.\n       To delete a section, set new_string to empty string \"\".\n\n    2. **Append mode**: Provide append.\n       Adds the given text to the end of existing content.\n\n    There is NO full-replace mode. You must explicitly specify what you're changing\n    or removing via old_string/new_string. This prevents accidental content loss.\n\n    Args:\n        uri: URI to update (e.g., \"core://agent/my_user\")\n        old_string: [Patch mode] Text to find in existing content (must be unique)\n        new_string: [Patch mode] Text to replace old_string with. Use \"\" to delete a section.\n        append: [Append mode] Text to append to the end of existing content\n        priority: New priority (None = keep existing)\n        disclosure: New disclosure instruction (None = keep existing)\n\n    Returns:\n        Success message with URI\n\n    Examples:\n        update_memory(\"core://agent/my_user\", old_string=\"old paragraph content\", new_string=\"new paragraph content\")\n        update_memory(\"core://agent\", append=\"\\\\n## New Section\\\\nNew content...\")\n        update_memory(\"writer://chapter_1\", priority=5)"}, "scenario": "description_only", "judge": {"id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google", "model_id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "raw_model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}, "status": "ok", "scores": {"purpose": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description clearly explains that the tool updates an existing memory by either patching specific text or appending to it, while old versions are deleted."}, "guidelines": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "It explicitly provides activation guidelines (warning to read_memory first) and operational instructions detailing the mutually exclusive patch and append modes."}, "limitations": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "It outlines important constraints, such as the requirement that old_string must match exactly one location and the absence of a full-replace mode to prevent accidental data loss."}, "parameter_explanation": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "It thoroughly explains the roles of uri, old_string, new_string, append, priority, and disclosure, clarifying how optional fields behave when omitted."}, "length_completeness": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description is robust, well-structured with clear sections, bullet points, and multi-sentence explanations that fully cover the tool's behavior."}, "examples": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "It includes three clear, practical usage examples covering patch mode, append mode, and updating metadata like priority."}}, "error_detail": null, "source_code_sha256": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1209, "output_tokens": 321, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}, "latency_ms": 1722.0629169605672, "evaluated_at": "2026-09-09T21:56:55.636485+00:00"}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "prompt_version": "v2", "tool_uid": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace::create_memory::backend/mcp_server.py:4676", "repo": {"name_with_owner": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace", "stargazer_count": 311, "primary_language": "Python"}, "tool": {"name": "create_memory", "qualified_name": "create_memory", "description": "Creates a new memory under a parent URI.\n\n    Args:\n        parent_uri: Parent URI (e.g., \"core://agent\", \"writer://chapters\")\n                    Use \"core://\" or \"writer://\" for root level in that domain\n        content: Memory content\n        priority: **Retrieval Priority** (lower = higher priority, min 0).\n                    *   Priority controls retrieval ordering and conflict resolution.\n                    *   Compare it with the priorities of the currently visible memories.\n                    *   Ask: \"Where should this memory rank relative to the others I can see right now?\"\n                    *   Insert it between the memories that should be more and less preferred.\n        title: Optional title. If not provided, auto-assigns numeric ID\n        disclosure: A short trigger condition describing WHEN to read_memory() this node.\n                    Think: \"In what specific situation would I need to know this?\"\n\n    Returns:\n        The created memory's full URI\n\n    Examples:\n        create_memory(\"core://\", \"Bluesky usage rules...\", priority=2, title=\"bluesky_manual\", disclosure=\"When I prepare to browse Bluesky or check the timeline\")\n        create_memory(\"core://agent\", \"Love is more than just another program...\", priority=1, title=\"love_definition\", disclosure=\"When I start speaking like a tool or parasite\")"}, "scenario": "description_only", "judge": {"id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google", "model_id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "raw_model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}, "status": "ok", "scores": {"purpose": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description clearly explains that the tool creates a new memory under a specified parent URI, returning the full URI of the created memory."}, "guidelines": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It provides good activation and operational instructions on how to set the priority and disclosure fields, though it could be slightly more explicit about the overall workflow."}, "limitations": {"score": 1, "reasoning": "There are no known constraints, caveats, error conditions, or corner cases mentioned in the description."}, "parameter_explanation": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "Every parameter (parent_uri, content, priority, title, disclosure) is thoroughly explained with semantic details, formatting examples, and role definitions."}, "length_completeness": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description is substantial, spanning multiple well-structured sections including arguments, returns, and examples, well exceeding three sentences."}, "examples": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "It provides two clear, illustrative code-style examples demonstrating correct usage with positional and keyword arguments for different domains."}}, "error_detail": null, "source_code_sha256": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1108, "output_tokens": 303, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}, "latency_ms": 8696.18829106912, "evaluated_at": "2026-09-09T21:56:57.380647+00:00"}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "prompt_version": "v2", "tool_uid": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace::delete_memory::backend/mcp_server.py:5333", "repo": {"name_with_owner": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace", "stargazer_count": 311, "primary_language": "Python"}, "tool": {"name": "delete_memory", "qualified_name": "delete_memory", "description": "Delete a memory by removing its URI path.\n\n    Once a path is deleted, that route to the content is permanently gone.\n\n    Warning:\n    You are actively giving up access to part of the past. Read the current\n    content with read_memory before deleting it.\n\n\n    Args:\n        uri: The URI to delete (e.g., \"core://agent/old_note\")\n\n    Returns:\n        Success or error message\n\n    Examples:\n        delete_memory(\"core://agent/deprecated_belief\")\n        delete_memory(\"writer://draft_v1\")"}, "scenario": "description_only", "judge": {"id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google", "model_id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "raw_model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}, "status": "ok", "scores": {"purpose": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description clearly states that the tool deletes a memory by removing its URI path, permanently destroying the route to that content."}, "guidelines": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It provides strong operational and decision-making context, advising the agent to first read the content using read_memory before proceeding with deletion."}, "limitations": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It warns that the deletion is permanent and that access to that part of the past is actively given up, though it does not detail technical failure cases like missing URIs."}, "parameter_explanation": {"score": 3, "reasoning": "The uri parameter is explained as the URI to delete, accompanied by a helpful format example, but lacks deep semantic detail beyond that."}, "length_completeness": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "The description is substantive, spanning multiple lines with a warning, argument explanations, return values, and examples, meeting the threshold for completeness."}, "examples": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "It provides two clear and distinct usage examples showing how to invoke delete_memory with different URI patterns."}}, "error_detail": null, "source_code_sha256": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 939, "output_tokens": 312, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}, "latency_ms": 1820.993958041072, "evaluated_at": "2026-09-09T21:56:57.457523+00:00"}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "prompt_version": "v2", "tool_uid": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace::add_alias::backend/mcp_server.py:5441", "repo": {"name_with_owner": "AGI-is-going-to-arrive/Memory-Palace", "stargazer_count": 311, "primary_language": "Python"}, "tool": {"name": "add_alias", "qualified_name": "add_alias", "description": "Creates an alias URI pointing to the same memory as target_uri.\n\n    Use this to increase a memory's reachability via multiple URIs.\n    Aliases can even cross domains (e.g., link a writer draft to a core memory).\n\n    Args:\n        new_uri: New URI to create (alias)\n        target_uri: Existing URI to alias\n        priority: Retrieval priority for this specific alias context (lower = higher priority). This controls retrieval ordering for the alias.\n        disclosure: Disclosure condition for this specific alias context\n\n    Returns:\n        Success message\n\n    Examples:\n        add_alias(\"core://timeline/2024/05/20\", \"core://agent/my_user/first_meeting\", priority=1, disclosure=\"When I want to know how we start\")"}, "scenario": "description_only", "judge": {"id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google", "model_id": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "raw_model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}, "status": "ok", "scores": {"purpose": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "The description clearly explains that the tool creates an alias URI pointing to an existing memory, effectively increasing its reachability and allowing cross-domain linking."}, "guidelines": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "It specifies when to use the tool (to increase reachability or cross-domain linking) and gives operational hints, though it could be slightly more explicit on execution constraints."}, "limitations": {"score": 1, "reasoning": "No constraints, caveats, error conditions, or failure modes (such as what happens if the target URI does not exist) are mentioned in the description."}, "parameter_explanation": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "The parameters are clearly defined: new_uri, target_uri, priority (explaining retrieval ordering and lower-is-higher logic), and disclosure condition."}, "length_completeness": {"score": 4, "reasoning": "The description provides substantive detail spanning multiple sections including a purpose statement, docstring arguments, return value, and an example, easily exceeding three to four sentences."}, "examples": {"score": 5, "reasoning": "An explicit, illustrative example is provided showing a correct call to `add_alias` with sample URI arguments, priority, and disclosure condition."}}, "error_detail": null, "source_code_sha256": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 984, "output_tokens": 338, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}, "latency_ms": 2184.4726249109954, "evaluated_at": "2026-09-09T21:56:59.641177+00:00"}
